# iOS / Swift Style

Swift files mirror the reference project's `services/apiClient.ts` shape.

## API client

- One `APIClient` struct with injectable `URLSession` and `baseURL`.
- Every method is `async throws` and either returns a decoded payload or
  throws an `APIClientError` case — no `Result`, no optionals-on-failure.
- Status-code check pattern (mirror the `if (!res.ok)` block in
  `apiClient.ts`):

```swift
guard (200 ... 299).contains(http.statusCode) else {
  throw APIClientError.badStatus(http.statusCode, bodyText)
}
```

## Error type

One `LocalizedError` enum per client. Every case has a concrete
`errorDescription` so the UI can surface `error.localizedDescription`
directly — no extra mapping at call-sites.

```swift
enum APIClientError: LocalizedError {
  case invalidURL
  case badStatus(Int, String)
  case decoding
  var errorDescription: String? { ... }
}
```

## Doc comments (intent, not mechanics)

Every public type and method gets a `///` comment describing **what it's
for** and any non-obvious constraint. This mirrors the JSDoc on every
exported function in `services/apiClient.ts`.

```swift
/// POST a batch of HealthKit samples to the server.
///
/// Returns silently on 2xx; throws `APIClientError` on any other outcome.
func uploadHealthData(_ payload: HealthUploadPayload) async throws { ... }
```

## Payload types

- One file per feature's payload group (e.g. `HealthUploadPayload.swift`).
- All payload structs are `Codable, Sendable`.
- Field names match the JSON contract exactly — no `CodingKeys`
  remapping unless the server demands it.
